::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does MLA.GEO.RP.4 require students to interpret and identify? {
=Conditional statements, converse, inverse, and contrapositive statements #Correct. The standard focuses on conditional logic and related statement forms.
~Only segment and angle constructions #Constructions belong to a different Geometry standard.
~Only coordinate distance formulas #Coordinate distance is not the focus of this logic standard.
~Only numerical equation solving #This lesson focuses on logical statements, not equation solving.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the conditional statement If a figure is a square, then it has four sides, what is the hypothesis? {
=A figure is a square #Correct. The hypothesis is the condition that follows if.
~It has four sides #This is the conclusion because it follows then.
~A figure is not a square #This negates the hypothesis.
~It does not have four sides #This negates the conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the conditional statement If a figure is a rectangle, then it has four right angles, what is the conclusion? {
~A figure is a rectangle #This is the hypothesis because it follows if.
=It has four right angles #Correct. The conclusion is the result that follows then.
~A figure is not a rectangle #This is the negation of the hypothesis.
~It does not have four right angles #This is the negation of the conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which form represents a conditional statement? {
~If q, then p #This is the converse form when starting from If p, then q.
~If not p, then not q #This is the inverse form when starting from If p, then q.
=If p, then q #Correct. This is the standard symbolic form of a conditional statement.
~If not q, then not p #This is the contrapositive form when starting from If p, then q.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the converse of If two angles are vertical angles, then they are congruent? {
~If two angles are not vertical angles, then they are not congruent #This is the inverse, not the converse.
=If two angles are congruent, then they are vertical angles #Correct. The converse switches the hypothesis and conclusion.
~If two angles are not congruent, then they are not vertical angles #This is the contrapositive, not the converse.
~If two angles are vertical angles, then they are not congruent #This changes the conclusion and is not the converse.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the inverse of If a figure is a triangle, then it has three sides? {
~If a figure has three sides, then it is a triangle #This is the converse, not the inverse.
=If a figure is not a triangle, then it does not have three sides #Correct. The inverse keeps the order and negates both parts.
~If a figure does not have three sides, then it is not a triangle #This is the contrapositive, not the inverse.
~If a figure is a triangle, then it does not have three sides #This negates only the conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the contrapositive of If a figure is a square, then it is a rectangle? {
~If a figure is a rectangle, then it is a square #This is the converse, not the contrapositive.
~If a figure is not a square, then it is not a rectangle #This is the inverse, not the contrapositive.
=If a figure is not a rectangle, then it is not a square #Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
~If a figure is a square, then it is not a rectangle #This negates only the conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is the converse of If p, then q? {
~If not p, then not q #This is the inverse.
=If q, then p #Correct. The converse switches p and q.
~If not q, then not p #This is the contrapositive.
~If p, then not q #This is not the converse.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is the inverse of If p, then q? {
=If not p, then not q #Correct. The inverse keeps the order and negates both parts.
~If q, then p #This is the converse.
~If not q, then not p #This is the contrapositive.
~If q, then not p #This is not the inverse form.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is the contrapositive of If p, then q? {
~If q, then p #This is the converse.
~If not p, then not q #This is the inverse.
=If not q, then not p #Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
~If p, then q #This is the original conditional.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does all mean in the statement All squares have four sides? {
~Only one square has four sides #All refers to every square, not one example.
=Every square has four sides #Correct. All means the statement applies to every object in the group.
~No square has four sides #This is the opposite of the statement.
~Some squares may not have four sides #All does not allow exceptions within the group.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which if-then statement matches All rectangles have four right angles? {
=If a figure is a rectangle, then it has four right angles #Correct. All rectangles means every rectangle has the stated property.
~If a figure has four right angles, then it is a rectangle #This is the converse of the matching conditional.
~If a figure is not a rectangle, then it does not have four right angles #This is the inverse.
~If a figure does not have four right angles, then it is not a rectangle #This is the contrapositive.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does not do in a related conditional statement? {
~It switches the order of p and q #Switching order creates the converse.
~It turns every statement into a biconditional #Not does not create an if and only if statement.
=It negates a condition or conclusion #Correct. Not denies or reverses the meaning of a statement part.
~It removes the hypothesis #The hypothesis remains a statement part even when negated.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does if and only if mean? {
~Only the conditional direction is included #If and only if includes both directions.
~Only the inverse direction is included #If and only if is not the inverse.
=Both the conditional and converse directions are included #Correct. A biconditional combines both directions.
~Both statement parts are negated #Negation is not what creates a biconditional.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair of statements is combined by p if and only if q? {
~If p, then q and if not p, then not q #This pairs the conditional with the inverse.
=If p, then q and if q, then p #Correct. A biconditional combines the conditional and converse.
~If not p, then not q and if not q, then not p #These are inverse and contrapositive forms.
~If p, then not q and if q, then not p #These do not represent a biconditional.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student says the inverse of If p, then q is If q, then p. What is the mistake? {
~The student wrote the contrapositive #The contrapositive is If not q, then not p.
=The student wrote the converse instead of the inverse #Correct. If q, then p switches the order without negating both parts.
~The student wrote the original conditional #The original conditional is If p, then q.
~The student wrote a biconditional #A biconditional uses if and only if.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student says the contrapositive of If p, then q is If not p, then not q. What is the mistake? {
~The student wrote the converse #The converse is If q, then p.
=The student wrote the inverse instead of the contrapositive #Correct. If not p, then not q is the inverse.
~The student wrote the original conditional #The original conditional is If p, then q.
~The student wrote a biconditional #A biconditional uses if and only if.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>For the conditional If a polygon has four sides, then it is a quadrilateral, which statement is the converse? {
~If a polygon does not have four sides, then it is not a quadrilateral #This is the inverse.
=If a polygon is a quadrilateral, then it has four sides #Correct. The converse switches the hypothesis and conclusion.
~If a polygon is not a quadrilateral, then it does not have four sides #This is the contrapositive.
~If a polygon has four sides, then it is not a quadrilateral #This negates only the conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>For the conditional If two lines are perpendicular, then they intersect, which statement is the inverse? {
~If two lines intersect, then they are perpendicular #This is the converse.
=If two lines are not perpendicular, then they do not intersect #Correct. The inverse keeps the order and negates both parts.
~If two lines do not intersect, then they are not perpendicular #This is the contrapositive.
~If two lines are perpendicular, then they do not intersect #This negates only the conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>For the conditional If an angle is acute, then its measure is less than 90°, which statement is the contrapositive? {
~If an angle measure is less than 90°, then the angle is acute #This is the converse.
~If an angle is not acute, then its measure is not less than 90° #This is the inverse.
=If an angle measure is not less than 90°, then the angle is not acute #Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
~If an angle is acute, then its measure is not less than 90° #This negates only the conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly identifies the hypothesis in If two segments are congruent, then they have equal length? {
=Two segments are congruent #Correct. This is the condition that follows if.
~They have equal length #This is the conclusion because it follows then.
~Two segments are not congruent #This is the negated hypothesis.
~They do not have equal length #This is the negated conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly identifies the conclusion in If a point is a midpoint, then it divides a segment into two equal parts? {
~A point is a midpoint #This is the hypothesis.
=It divides a segment into two equal parts #Correct. This is the result that follows then.
~A point is not a midpoint #This is the negated hypothesis.
~It does not divide a segment into two equal parts #This is the negated conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which related statement keeps the order p then q but negates both parts? {
~Converse #The converse switches order without negating both parts.
=Inverse #Correct. The inverse is If not p, then not q.
~Contrapositive #The contrapositive switches order and negates both parts.
~Biconditional #A biconditional uses if and only if.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which related statement switches the order and negates both parts? {
~Converse #The converse switches order but does not negate both parts.
~Inverse #The inverse negates both parts but keeps the order.
=Contrapositive #Correct. The contrapositive is If not q, then not p.
~Conditional #The original conditional is If p, then q.
}

::GEO_U01_L06_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L06_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which explanation best corrects the error Converse: If not p, then not q? {
~The converse should negate only q #Negating only q does not create the converse.
~The converse should negate only p #Negating only p does not create the converse.
~The converse should be If not q, then not p #That is the contrapositive, not the converse.
=The converse should be If q, then p because it switches the original parts #Correct. The converse reverses p and q without adding not.
}
